## Summary

Audited main at 4185a16446f88ee5d1da3f2cec026b337965467d against the documented trust model and prior security findings. Fixed one verified authorization bypass at the shared data-model write boundary.

Before: a signed-in member who knew a private CONTENT row ID could insert a PHOTO they owned with that ID in reencode, crop or thumbnail, making the private bytes readable through the new photo. Unreadable originals could also enter privileged background media processing.
Now: Model.Authorize checks every newly assigned CONTENT reference against the caller's read permissions before the write. Upload handlers carry an internal, unexported attestation for the exact content supplied, preserving uploads and deduplication even when the same private bytes already exist. Clients cannot supply this attestation through JSON. Destination write permissions remain enforced, and system jobs and super admins retain their documented access.

The fix covers all writable content references centrally rather than adding individual endpoint checks. Upload and wiki handlers provide the narrow internal attestation. Added regression tests for inserts, updates, forged attestations, readable reuse and duplicate private-byte uploads; updated two ownership-test fixtures to use readable content; documented the rule in docs/datamodel.md. Non-super-admin API callers attempting to attach unreadable content by ID must instead upload bytes they possess or select readable content.

Deliberately retained documented public previews, tokens in trusted project logs (prior finding #61 invalid), and short-lived PKCE-bound OAuth code reuse. Knowledge of a private content ID is required for the demonstrated exploit; content-ID enumeration was not established. No production deployment, data migration or GitHub issue creation.

